annual increase, even as the company suffered a sharp decline in operating profit on reduced deliveries of its G650 aircraft. But new orders\u2014including a $9.5 billion contract to build Columbia-class submarines\u2014and a record $89.5 billion order backlog point to strong growth potential.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:14723,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;4&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:16777215},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;14&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:0},&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">The company behind the Gulfstream jet and the M1A1 Abrams tank largely dodged the worst of the COVID-19 disruptions. In March, the General Dynamics board agreed to hike the quarterly dividend, the 24th consecutive annual increase, even as the company suffered a sharp decline in operating profit on reduced deliveries of its G650 aircraft. But new orders\u2014including a $9.5 billion contract to build Columbia-class submarines\u2014and a record $89.5 billion order backlog point to strong growth potential.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 84, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;The company behind the Gulfstream jet and the M1A1 Abrams tank largely dodged the worst of the COVID-19 disruptions. In March, the General Dynamics board agreed to hike the quarterly dividend, the 24th consecutive annual increase, even as the company suffered a sharp decline in operating profit on reduced deliveries of its G650 aircraft. But new orders\u2014including a $9.5 billion contract to build Columbia-class submarines\u2014and a record $89.5 billion order backlog point to strong growth potential.&quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:14723,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;4&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:16777215},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;14&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:0},&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">The company behind the Gulfstream jet and the M1A1 Abrams tank largely dodged the worst of the COVID-19 disruptions. In March, the General Dynamics board agreed to hike the quarterly dividend, the 24th consecutive annual increase, even as the company suffered a sharp decline in operating profit on reduced deliveries of its G650 aircraft. But new orders\u2014including a $9.5 billion contract to build Columbia-class submarines\u2014and a record $89.5 billion order backlog point to strong growth potential.</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 317, "itemDescription": "Revenues at General Dynamics were up 8.7% in 2019, compared with the previous year, to $39.4 billion; profits, though, lagged compared with some competitors', growing just 4.2%. One big win was in tanks and weapons systems, where sales were up 12.3% for the year. That uptick came despite a diplomatic imbroglio between Canada\u2019s government and Saudi Arabia that delayed payment on part of a $13 billion contract to sell armored vehicles to Saudi Arabia. (The dispute was resolved in April 2020.) But GD\u2019s information technology segment stalled, posting a mere 1.9% growth. So far, 2020 is off to a mixed start: Coronavirus-related travel restrictions and production problems hammered GD\u2019s first-quarter aerospace revenues, especially the core Gulfstream business jet line, while IT sales slumped even further."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 83, "itemDescription": "General Dynamics climbed nine places on the <em>Fortune</em> 500 this year, quite an accomplishment for a company that\u2019s been on the list since its inception. Revenues were up 8.7%, year-over-year, to $39.4 billion; profits, though, lagged some competitors', growing just 4.2% for the year. One big win was in tanks and weapons systems, where sales were up 12.3% for the year. That uptick came despite a diplomatic imbroglio between Canada\u2019s government and Saudi Arabia that delayed payment on part of a $13 billion contract to sell armored vehicles to Saudi Arabia. (The dispute was resolved in April 2020.) But GD\u2019s information technology segment stalled out at just 1.9% growth. So far, 2020 is off to a mixed start: Coronavirus-related travel restrictions and production problems hammered GD\u2019s first-quarter aerospace revenues, especially the core Gulfstream business jet line, while IT sales slumped even further."}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2019, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 343, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 92, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2018, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 383, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 99, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2017, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 345, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 90, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2016, "rank": null, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2016, "rank": 330, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2016, "rank": 88, "itemDescrip